<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commonwealth of Independent States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as CIS</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) is a regional organisation of former Soviet republics, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a> of 8 December 1991 and joined by most other republics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Alma-Ata</a> later that month. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as a framework for cooperation, but with limited powers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> left in 2009 and Ukraine ended its participation in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states.jpg" alt="Commonwealth of Independent States" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Denelson83 at Wikimedia Commons. Redrawn from scratch by MapGrid in 2021. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_CIS.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…joined the United Nations on 2 March 1992 and belongs to the Council of Europe, the OSCE, the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b> and the Eurasian Economic Union, which it chose in 2013 over an EU association agreement before signing the Comprehensive and Enhanced Partnership Agreement with the EU…</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It holds diplomatic relations with 182 countries and belongs to 38 international organizations, among them the United Nations, the Council of Europe since 2001, the OSCE and NATO's Partnership for Peace; it was a founder of GUAM, the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b> and the OPCW.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 December 1991 Yeltsin and the leaders of Ukraine and Belarus signed the Belovezha Accords, declaring that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and founding the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 December the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus signed the Belovezha Accords, declaring that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and founding the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Uzbekistan joined the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b> in December 1991 and the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and belongs to the OSCE, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the Organisation of Turkic States and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation; it joined GUAM in 1999 and left in 2005, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting in the Belovezha Forest, Boris Yeltsin, Leonid Kravchuk and Stanislav Shushkevich declared that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and created the <b>Commonwealth of Independent States</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
